grouping would keep merging them into one bubble (good for streaming).  \n- Option 1 (new bubble every time) would split them (bad).\n\nSo if the gateway ever starts true streaming deltas for a single message, we’d want to preserve the merge behavior. One clean way to do that is to keep the `(runID, index)` key but also track the `messageId` (or a 1:1 mapping) and only merge when the `messageId` is identical *and* we’re in a streaming context. That’s essentially a hybrid of A and C.\n\nFor now, since the observed wire traffic is complete messages (one `session.message` → multiple `messageDelta` with distinct indices), A 1:1 matches the current UX and keeps the diff tiny.\n\nOn the config path question: yes, `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH` can point to a file that contains `logging.file` (and any other top-level keys). The gateway just reads the JSON and merges it into the runtime config; no special casing needed.\n\nAs for `/tmp/openclaw` vs `~/.openclaw`:\n\n- Literal scope-lock wording: `/tmp/openclaw` is *not* the state directory (`STATE_DIR` is `~/.openclaw`). So the literal reading of condition ② might still pass.  \n- Intent / spirit: it *is 100 % a shared user-environment openclaw path*, and the recipe §5 explicitly calls out `/tmp/openclaw` as the “preferred temp root” rather than the state dir. That’s the same class of looseness that got dinged in T-080.\n\nMy ruling:  \n- (a) `/tmp/open claw` is **not** the technical `STATE_DIR`, but it **is** a shared openclaw path and violates the isolation intent.  \n- (b) Means exists: yes, `logging.file` in the config.  \n- (c) Narrowing the criterion to exclude `/tmp/openclaw` would be 0011-style re-interpretation.\n\nRecommendation: keep the literal reading, mark the `/tmp/openclaw` usage as a violation of spirit, and require `logging.file` in the isolated config to satisfy both literal and intent. That’s the cleanest fix.I have everything I need. Let me also quickly check one subtle attack on A regarding multi-block:\nThat 